# Drag along the rail to scrub, the way you would drag a scrollbar.
#
# Clicking a mark already worked (minimap_navigator.e2e). What this holds is
# the gesture: press, sweep, release — and the three things that are easy to
# get wrong and invisible in a screenshot, because every one of them needs the
# button to still be down.
#
#   1. The transcript follows DURING the drag, not on release. Asserted
#      between a mouse_down and its mouse_up, which is the only place that
#      claim can be made.
#   2. Releasing OUTSIDE the rail ends the drag. A gesture that ended by
#      hit-testing the cursor instead of watching the button would leave the
#      drag latched, and the next idle mouse move would scrub the thread —
#      step 5 is that test, and it is why the release happens off the rail.
#   3. A press that never moves is still a click on the mark it landed on.
#
# The rail is at x=1084..1094, y=73..656 at this window size (measured with
# `assert_ui minimap_rail x=…`), so 1089 is its centre line. Coordinates
# rather than `click_ui minimap_mark_N` because the runner has no
# element-addressed press or move — every *_ui input command is a click
# (afterhours_gaps.md #285).
#
# The two ends of the scroll range are the deterministic assertions here: any
# large upward sweep clamps at the top of the thread, where question #0 is,
# whatever the exact px-per-message scale works out to at this window size. A
# middle position would be pinning arithmetic that the unit test
# (tests/unit/test_minimap_scrub.cpp) already pins properly.
#
# NOTE: expect_no_text takes a BARE single word — see afterhours_gaps.md #47.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":[],"active_tab":"","theme":"dark"}
# env: HANABI_BIG_TRANSCRIPT=1 HANABI_OPEN=rbig

# Opens at the end: the first question is far above the viewport.
expect_no_text #0:

# 1. Press low on the rail. That is a click, and it lands somewhere late.
mouse_down 1089 650
wait_frames 8
expect_no_text #0:

# 2. Still held: sweep up the rail. A short first step, because the injector
#    teleports the cursor and the 6px drag threshold would otherwise swallow a
#    whole long move. Then a long sweep past the rail's top edge, which the
#    scroll clamp turns into "the top of the thread".
mouse_move 1089 620
wait_frames 4
mouse_move 1089 300
wait_frames 6
mouse_move 1089 5
wait_frames 12
expect_text "Follow-up question #0:"

# 3. Leave the rail sideways — a real hand does — and release out there.
mouse_move 300 5
wait_frames 6
mouse_up
wait_frames 8
expect_text "Follow-up question #0:"

# 4. The release ENDED it. Sweeping back down the rail with the button up
#    moves nothing; a latched drag would have scrubbed to the far end.
mouse_move 1089 650
wait_frames 14
expect_text "Follow-up question #0:"

# 5. And a press that never moves is still the click it always was.
mouse_down 1089 300
wait_frames 6
mouse_up
wait_frames 14
expect_no_text #0:
